    "['route-policy [parameter1] permit node [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept'}]]": 1,
    "['apply mpls-label', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 then accept', 'parent_command': ['set policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept'], 'root': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 then accept\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 then next-hop self\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent of Huawei\\'s \"apply mpls-label\" in Juniper isn\\'t a single command. In Juniper, you would typically use a policy that accepts the route and sets the next-hop to self (which implicitly enables label allocation for BGP routes). If you specifically need MPLS label manipulation, you might need additional commands depending on the context.\\n\\nFor a more precise translation if the intent is to advertise labeled routes:\\n##\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 then family inet labeled-unicast\\n##\\n\\nChoose the appropriate version based on your specific requirement. The first option is more commonly used when translating Huawei configurations.', 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 then accept'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 then next-hop self', 'parent_command': ['set policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept'], 'root': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 then accept\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 then next-hop self\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent of Huawei\\'s \"apply mpls-label\" in Juniper isn\\'t a single command. In Juniper, you would typically use a policy that accepts the route and sets the next-hop to self (which implicitly enables label allocation for BGP routes). If you specifically need MPLS label manipulation, you might need additional commands depending on the context.\\n\\nFor a more precise translation if the intent is to advertise labeled routes:\\n##\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 then family inet labeled-unicast\\n##\\n\\nChoose the appropriate version based on your specific requirement. The first option is more commonly used when translating Huawei configurations.', 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 then next-hop self'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 then family inet labeled-unicast', 'parent_command': ['set policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept'], 'root': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 term 10 then accept', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 then accept\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 then next-hop self\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent of Huawei\\'s \"apply mpls-label\" in Juniper isn\\'t a single command. In Juniper, you would typically use a policy that accepts the route and sets the next-hop to self (which implicitly enables label allocation for BGP routes). If you specifically need MPLS label manipulation, you might need additional commands depending on the context.\\n\\nFor a more precise translation if the intent is to advertise labeled routes:\\n##\\nset policy-options policy-statement p1 then family inet labeled-unicast\\n##\\n\\nChoose the appropriate version based on your specific requirement. The first option is more commonly used when translating Huawei configurations.', 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement p1 then family inet labeled-unicast'}]]": 1,
